@@@ section
{"id": "strings", "title": "Strings & Bytes", "summary": "C strings are just bytes ending in '\\0'. Learn to walk, copy and reshape them without stepping outside the buffer."}

@@@ problem string-length
@@ meta
{
  "title": "String Length",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["Strings", "Pointers"],
  "params": [{"name": "s", "type": "string"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["\"hello\""], "output": "5", "explanation": "The string occupies 6 bytes: 'h','e','l','l','o' and the terminating '\\0'. The length counts only the first 5."},
    {"input": ["\"\""], "output": "0", "explanation": "The empty string is a single '\\0' byte."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Keep reading characters until you reach the byte '\\0'.",
    "With a pointer: advance p while *p != '\\0'; the answer is p - s."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= strlen(s) <= 10^4", "s is NUL-terminated"]
}
@@ description
Implement your own version of `strlen`: return the number of characters in `s` **before** the terminating null byte `'\0'`.

```c
size_t myStrlen(const char *s);
```

A C string has no stored length. It is just a sequence of `char`s that ends with a byte whose value is `0`. Every string function in the standard library finds the end the same way you will: by scanning for that byte.

> **Memory check:** the string is placed so that its `'\0'` is the very last byte of its block. Reading one byte further is reported as a heap buffer overflow.
@@ prelude
size_t myStrlen(const char *s);
@@ starter
#include <stddef.h>

size_t myStrlen(const char *s) {
    // TODO: count the characters before the terminating '\0'.
    return 0;
}
@@ solution
#include <stddef.h>

size_t myStrlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p != '\0')
        p++;
    return (size_t)(p - s);
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *s = lc_read_str();
        lc_make_readonly(s);
        lc_out_int((long long)myStrlen(s));
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
"C"

"memory management"

"tabs\tand\nnewlines"

"   "

"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog."
@@ editorial
## Approach: scan for the terminator

```c
size_t myStrlen(const char *s) {
    const char *p = s;
    while (*p != '\0')
        p++;
    return (size_t)(p - s);   // pointer difference = number of chars passed
}
```

Subtracting two pointers into the same array gives the number of **elements** between them. Here the elements are `char`s, so it is exactly the length.

### Why `size_t`?
`size_t` is the unsigned type C uses for sizes and lengths. It is 64 bits wide here, so it can describe any object in memory. Print it with `%zu`.

### What strlen costs
`strlen` is **O(n)**: it has to walk the whole string every time. A loop like `for (i = 0; i < strlen(s); i++)` re-scans the string on every iteration and becomes O(n²). Compute the length once and store it.

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) extra memory.

@@@ problem reverse-a-string
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Reverse a String In Place",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["Strings", "Two pointers"],
  "params": [{"name": "s", "type": "string"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["\"hello\""], "output": "\"olleh\""},
    {"input": ["\"ab\""], "output": "\"ba\""},
    {"input": ["\"\""], "output": "\"\""}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Find the length first, then swap characters from both ends toward the middle.",
    "The '\\0' must stay where it is: reverse only the characters before it."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= strlen(s) <= 10^4", "s is a writable buffer"]
}
@@ description
Reverse the string `s` **in place**: rearrange its characters inside the buffer you were given. The terminating `'\0'` stays at the end.

```c
void reverseString(char *s);
```

The harness prints `s` after your function returns.

Note that `s` is `char *`, not `const char *`: this time you are allowed, and expected, to modify the caller's memory.
@@ prelude
void reverseString(char *s);
@@ starter
#include <string.h>

void reverseString(char *s) {
    // TODO: swap characters from both ends toward the middle.
}
@@ solution
#include <string.h>

void reverseString(char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    if (n < 2) return;
    char *lo = s, *hi = s + n - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        char tmp = *lo;
        *lo++ = *hi;
        *hi-- = tmp;
    }
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *s = lc_read_str();
        reverseString(s);
        lc_out_str(s);
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
"a"

"racecar"

"LeakCode!"

"12345 67890"

"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
@@ editorial
## Approach: two pointers over the characters

```c
void reverseString(char *s) {
    size_t n = strlen(s);
    if (n < 2) return;
    char *lo = s, *hi = s + n - 1;   // last character, not the '\0'
    while (lo < hi) {
        char tmp = *lo;
        *lo++ = *hi;
        *hi-- = tmp;
    }
}
```

The key detail is `hi = s + n - 1`: `s + n` is the `'\0'`. Swapping it to the front would turn the result into an empty string.

### Writable vs read-only strings
```c
char a[] = "hello";   // an array: a writable copy of the literal
char *b = "hello";    // a pointer to the literal itself: read-only!
reverseString(a);     // fine
reverseString(b);     // undefined behavior: usually a crash
```
String literals live in read-only static memory. Only modify strings you own: arrays, or blocks from `malloc`.

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(1) extra memory.

@@@ problem bytes-of-an-integer
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Bytes of an Integer",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "topics": ["Bytes", "Bit manipulation", "Endianness"],
  "params": [{"name": "value", "type": "long"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["305419896"], "output": "[[120,86,52,18],305419896]", "explanation": "305419896 is 0x12345678. Little-endian order stores the lowest byte first: 0x78 (120), 0x56 (86), 0x34 (52), 0x12 (18)."},
    {"input": ["1"], "output": "[[1,0,0,0],1]"}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The lowest byte is value & 0xFF. The next one is (value >> 8) & 0xFF.",
    "To rebuild the number, shift each byte back into place and combine with |. Cast to uint32_t before shifting."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= value <= 2^32 - 1"]
}
@@ description
Every object in memory is a sequence of bytes. A `uint32_t` occupies 4 of them, and a CPU must choose an order to store them in. x86 and ARM computers are **little-endian**: the least significant byte comes first.

Write two functions:

```c
void toLittleEndian(uint32_t value, unsigned char out[4]);
uint32_t fromLittleEndian(const unsigned char bytes[4]);
```

- `toLittleEndian` stores the 4 bytes of `value` into `out`, least significant first.
- `fromLittleEndian` does the reverse: it combines 4 little-endian bytes back into a number.

The harness prints `[bytes, n]`, where `bytes` is what `toLittleEndian` produced and `n` is the result of `fromLittleEndian` applied to the correct bytes of `value`.

Use shifts and masks rather than relying on the machine's byte order: that way your code is correct on any computer.
@@ prelude
#include <stdint.h>
void toLittleEndian(uint32_t value, unsigned char out[4]);
uint32_t fromLittleEndian(const unsigned char bytes[4]);
@@ starter
#include <stdint.h>

void toLittleEndian(uint32_t value, unsigned char out[4]) {
    // TODO: out[0] is the least significant byte.
}

uint32_t fromLittleEndian(const unsigned char bytes[4]) {
    // TODO: combine the 4 bytes back into one number.
    return 0;
}
@@ solution
#include <stdint.h>

void toLittleEndian(uint32_t value, unsigned char out[4]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        out[i] = (unsigned char)((value >> (8 * i)) & 0xFF);
}

uint32_t fromLittleEndian(const unsigned char bytes[4]) {
    uint32_t v = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        v |= (uint32_t)bytes[i] << (8 * i);
    return v;
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        uint32_t value = (uint32_t)lc_read_ll();
        unsigned char *out = (unsigned char *)lc_harness_alloc(4);
        unsigned char *in = (unsigned char *)lc_harness_alloc(4);
        int i;
        memset(out, 0xAA, 4);
        toLittleEndian(value, out);
        for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) in[i] = (unsigned char)(value >> (8 * i));
        lc_make_readonly(in);
        lc_out_begin();
        lc_out_begin();
        for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) lc_out_int(out[i]);
        lc_out_end();
        lc_out_int((long long)fromLittleEndian(in));
        lc_out_end();
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
0

255

256

65535

4294967295

3735928559

2147483648
@@ editorial
## Approach: shifts and masks

```c
void toLittleEndian(uint32_t value, unsigned char out[4]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        out[i] = (unsigned char)((value >> (8 * i)) & 0xFF);
}

uint32_t fromLittleEndian(const unsigned char bytes[4]) {
    uint32_t v = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        v |= (uint32_t)bytes[i] << (8 * i);
    return v;
}
```

`value >> 8*i` moves byte `i` down to the lowest position and `& 0xFF` keeps only those 8 bits.

### Why cast before shifting?
`bytes[i]` is promoted to `int` before the shift. `0xDE << 24` overflows a signed `int`, which is undefined behavior. Casting to `uint32_t` first makes the shift well defined.

### Looking at the bytes directly
You can also inspect an object's bytes through an `unsigned char *`, which C always allows:
```c
uint32_t x = 0x12345678;
unsigned char *p = (unsigned char *)&x;
// on a little-endian machine: p[0] == 0x78
```
or copy them with `memcpy(out, &value, 4)`. Both give the **machine's** order, so they are only "little-endian" on little-endian hardware. Network protocols and file formats specify an order, which is why shift-based code is the portable choice.

**Complexity:** O(1).

@@@ problem safe-string-copy
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Safe String Copy",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "topics": ["Strings", "Buffers", "Bounds"],
  "params": [{"name": "src", "type": "string"}, {"name": "dstSize", "type": "int"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["\"hello\"", "10"], "output": "[\"hello\",5]", "explanation": "Everything fits: 5 characters plus '\\0' need 6 of the 10 bytes."},
    {"input": ["\"hello\"", "4"], "output": "[\"hel\",5]", "explanation": "Only 3 characters fit next to the '\\0'. The return value 5 >= 4 tells the caller the copy was truncated."},
    {"input": ["\"hello\"", "0"], "output": "[null,5]", "explanation": "With a 0-byte buffer nothing may be written at all (the harness prints null for the empty buffer)."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "You may copy at most dstSize - 1 characters, then you must write the '\\0'.",
    "Handle dstSize == 0 first: then dst has no room even for the terminator.",
    "The return value is always strlen(src), whether or not everything fit."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= strlen(src) <= 1000", "0 <= dstSize <= 1000", "dst points to exactly dstSize writable bytes"]
}
@@ description
`strcpy(dst, src)` has no idea how big `dst` is: if `src` is too long, it writes past the end. It is the source of countless security bugs. Implement a **bounded** copy instead (the BSD function `strlcpy` works this way):

```c
size_t safeCopy(char *dst, size_t dstSize, const char *src);
```

- `dst` points to a buffer of exactly `dstSize` bytes.
- Copy as much of `src` as fits, and **always** terminate `dst` with `'\0'` (when `dstSize > 0`).
- Never write more than `dstSize` bytes.
- Return `strlen(src)`. The caller detects truncation with `safeCopy(...) >= dstSize`.

The harness prints `[dst, returnValue]`.

> **Memory check:** `dst` is allocated with exactly `dstSize` bytes. Writing even one byte too many is reported as a heap buffer overflow, and a `dst` without its `'\0'` crashes the harness when it reads your result.
@@ prelude
size_t safeCopy(char *dst, size_t dstSize, const char *src);
@@ starter
#include <stddef.h>

size_t safeCopy(char *dst, size_t dstSize, const char *src) {
    // TODO: copy at most dstSize - 1 chars, always terminate, return strlen(src).
    return 0;
}
@@ solution
#include <stddef.h>

size_t safeCopy(char *dst, size_t dstSize, const char *src) {
    size_t len = 0;
    while (src[len] != '\0')
        len++;
    if (dstSize > 0) {
        size_t n = len < dstSize - 1 ? len : dstSize - 1;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
            dst[i] = src[i];
        dst[n] = '\0';
    }
    return len;
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *src = lc_read_str();
        int size = lc_read_int();
        char *dst;
        size_t ret;
        if (size < 0) lc_abort_case("Invalid test case", "dstSize must not be negative.");
        dst = (char *)lc_harness_alloc((size_t)size);
        lc_make_readonly(src);
        ret = safeCopy(dst, (size_t)size, src);
        lc_out_begin();
        if (size > 0) lc_out_str(dst);
        else lc_out_null();
        lc_out_int((long long)ret);
        lc_out_end();
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
@@ tests
"hello"
6

"hello"
5

"hello"
1

""
4

""
0

"memory"
3

"A longer source string that will certainly not fit"
16
@@ editorial
## Approach: measure, then copy what fits

```c
size_t safeCopy(char *dst, size_t dstSize, const char *src) {
    size_t len = 0;
    while (src[len] != '\0')
        len++;
    if (dstSize > 0) {
        size_t n = len < dstSize - 1 ? len : dstSize - 1;   // room for the '\0'
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
            dst[i] = src[i];
        dst[n] = '\0';
    }
    return len;
}
```

### The `dstSize == 0` trap
`dstSize` is a `size_t`, which is unsigned. With `dstSize == 0`, `dstSize - 1` is not -1 but **18446744073709551615**, and a careless loop would happily write that many bytes. The `if (dstSize > 0)` check comes first for exactly this reason.

### Why return `strlen(src)`?
The caller learns both "did it fit?" (`ret < dstSize`) and "how big a buffer would I need?" (`ret + 1`). `snprintf` reports truncation the same way.

### And `strncpy`?
Despite its name, `strncpy(dst, src, n)` is **not** a safe `strcpy`: when `src` has `n` or more characters it leaves `dst` without a terminator. Prefer `snprintf(dst, size, "%s", src)` in real code.

**Complexity:** O(len(src)) time, O(1) extra memory.

@@@ problem implement-memmove
@@ meta
{
  "title": "Implement memmove",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "topics": ["Memory", "void pointers", "Overlap"],
  "params": [{"name": "buf", "type": "string"}, {"name": "dst", "type": "int"}, {"name": "src", "type": "int"}, {"name": "n", "type": "int"}],
  "examples": [
    {"input": ["\"abcdefgh\"", "2", "0", "5"], "output": "\"ababcdeh\"", "explanation": "Copy the 5 bytes \"abcde\" to offset 2. The ranges overlap, and the destination is after the source."},
    {"input": ["\"abcdefgh\"", "0", "2", "5"], "output": "\"cdefgfgh\"", "explanation": "Copy \"cdefg\" to offset 0. The destination is before the source."},
    {"input": ["\"hello world\"", "6", "0", "5"], "output": "\"hello hello\"", "explanation": "No overlap: any copy order works."}
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Convert the void pointers to unsigned char pointers so you can copy byte by byte.",
    "If you copy front to back while the destination is after the source, you overwrite bytes before reading them.",
    "When dst > src, copy from the last byte down to the first."
  ],
  "constraints": ["0 <= n", "0 <= dst + n <= strlen(buf)", "0 <= src + n <= strlen(buf)"]
}
@@ description
Implement `memmove`: copy `n` bytes from `src` to `dst` and return `dst`. Unlike `memcpy`, the two regions **may overlap**, and the result must be as if the bytes were first copied to a temporary buffer.

```c
void *myMemmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n);
```

Both regions are inside the same buffer `buf`. The harness calls `myMemmove(buf + dst, buf + src, n)` and prints the buffer afterwards. It also checks that you return `dst`.

`void *` is a generic pointer: it can hold the address of anything, but you can't dereference it or do arithmetic on it. Convert it to `unsigned char *` to work with raw bytes.

Don't call `memmove` or `memcpy`, and don't allocate a temporary buffer: copy in the right direction instead.
@@ prelude
void *myMemmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n);
@@ starter
#include <stddef.h>

void *myMemmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n) {
    // TODO: copy n bytes; the regions may overlap.
    return dst;
}
@@ solution
#include <stddef.h>

void *myMemmove(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n) {
    unsigned char *d = dst;
    const unsigned char *s = src;
    if (d < s) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
            d[i] = s[i];
    } else if (d > s) {
        for (size_t i = n; i > 0; i--)
            d[i - 1] = s[i - 1];
    }
    return dst;
}
@@ driver
#include "lc_driver.h"

int main(void)
{
    while (lc_next_case()) {
        char *buf = lc_read_str();
        int d = lc_read_int(), s = lc_read_int(), n = lc_read_int();
        int len = (int)strlen(buf);
        long long before;
        void *ret;
        if (n < 0 || d < 0 || s < 0 || d + n > len || s + n > len)
            lc_abort_case("Invalid test case", "Both ranges must lie inside buf.");
        before = lc_alloc_count();
        ret = myMemmove(buf + d, buf + s, (size_t)n);
        if (ret != buf + d) lc_fail("myMemmove must return dst.");
        if (lc_alloc_count() != before) lc_fail("myMemmove allocated memory. Copy in the right direction instead of using a temporary buffer.");
        lc_out_str(buf);
        lc_end_case();
    }
    return lc_finish();
}
